Extension: Inverse Application of TDMU Spectroscopy — Inferring Weaving Structure from Fine Spectra

If TDMU is valid, spectroscopy transforms from a “phenomenological taxonomy” into a “weaving-structure tomography”—using light as a probe to scan layer by layer the internal weaving history of the atomic nucleus. The following is a rigorous extension based on the mechanisms established in the preceding paper.

1. Basic Principle: Spectral Lines as Weaving Fingerprints

According to TDMU spectroscopy, each characteristic spectral line corresponds to the geometric properties of a specific linkage zone (weaving scar or topological defect). The three geometric parameters of a linkage zone—depth, degree of stability, and smoothness—are completely encoded in the frequency, width, and intensity of the spectral line.

Thus, the set of spectral lines = a faithful mapping of the set of linkage zones within the weave body. Measuring all spectral lines, in principle, yields a holographic projection of the internal geometric structure of the weave body.

